WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.140
Cameos, classic cars, and a made-up brand
of cigarettes…

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.620
Before the MCU, there was the Tarantino-verse,
a world jam-packed with easter eggs that you

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:09.620
won’t want to miss.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.530
Quentin Tarantino is fond of bringing recurring
elements across his filmography, with one

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.410
of the most famous examples being fictional
brands.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:21.110
One of the most prominent is Red Apple Cigarettes:
introduced in "Pulp Fiction," the fictional

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:25.000
cigarette brand is smoked by several different
characters in the 1994 film.

00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:27.378
"A pack of red apples."

00:00:27.378 --> 00:00:28.378
"Filters?"

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:29.519
"None."

00:00:29.842 --> 00:00:33.630
The cigarette brand also makes appearances
in "The Hateful Eight" and the Tarantino-helmed

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.930
sequence from 1995's anthology film "Four
Rooms."

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.930
Other notable in-film advertising for Red
Apple Cigarettes include a Japanese billboard

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.250
glimpsed in "Kill Bill: Volume 1" and an end
credits scene from "Once Upon a Time... in Hollywood."

00:00:46.523 --> 00:00:50.640
One of the big mysteries of "Kill Bill: Volume
1" revolved around the name of Uma Thurman's

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.829
vengeful protagonist, referred to in the film
and its marketing as "The Bride."

00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:00.930
Whenever the character's name was mentioned
in "Volume 1", it was audibly censored to

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.390
maintain the mystery.

00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:03.435
"Hi, honey.

00:01:04.747 --> 00:01:05.773
I'm -BLOOP-.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.518
What's your name?"

00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:11.490
However, The Bride's full name is revealed
in "Volume 1," long before the villainous

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.549
Elle Driver announces it in "Volume 2."

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:16.689
You just need to be really focusing on the
screen to see it.

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:20.549
After regaining consciousness in an El Paso
hospital, The Bride orders a one-way plane

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:25.875
ticket to travel to Okinawa, intending to
obtain a katana from retired sword maker Hattori Hanzo.

00:01:25.875 --> 00:01:29.579
In a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment, the
printed plane ticket clearly has the name

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:31.970
"Beatrix Kiddo " displayed in the top corner.

00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:38.310
Tarantino made his feature-length directorial
debut with 1992's "Reservoir Dogs," a relatively

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.370
low-budget crime film following a group of
thieves in the aftermath of a disastrous jewelry

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:43.370
store heist.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.810
One of the many elements that made the film
stand out was Tarantino's decision not to

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.940
show the actual robbery.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.340
In a flashback, viewers briefly glimpse the
chaotic aftermath, and judging by a mistake

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.187
in that sequence, perhaps it's better that
most of the film is dialogue inside a nondescript warehouse.

00:01:57.187 --> 00:02:01.121
As Mr. Pink recounts how he barely escaped
from the police after the botched heist, he

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:05.610
can be seen sprinting down a busy Los Angeles
street with the cops in pursuit on foot.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.330
After pushing his way past two pedestrians,
the film crew's reflection is visible in a

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:13.250
storefront window in the background, from
the camera crew themselves to a boom mic used

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.690
to capture the chaotic audio.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.470
A seasoned filmmaker likely would have seen
such potential for error and employed some

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:22.980
kind of obfuscation technique — but since
Tarantino was a rookie, his mistake survived

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.160
the final cut.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.465
In a way, perhaps that exemplifies the hurried
thrill of early-'90s indie cinema.

00:02:28.904 --> 00:02:33.410
Tarantino has included nods and homages to
spaghetti westerns in his work for years,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.300
and leaned into these influences for his own
"Django Unchained."

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.260
The 2012 film stars Jamie Foxx as the titular
freed slave, determined to rescue his wife

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.910
Broomhilda from the sadistic plantation owner
Calvin Candie.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.340
Posing as a man interested in buying a slave
for gladiatorial fights, Django meets with

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.470
Candie — who is in competition with a rival
slaveowner, played by veteran spaghetti western

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.080
actor Franco Nero.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.300
Nero played the original version of the character
in the 1966 spaghetti western film "Django."

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.159
In a visual reference to Nero's spaghetti
western, he wears bright white gloves — a

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:07.430
nod to Nero's iteration of Django having his
hands maimed over the course of the 1966 film.

00:03:07.713 --> 00:03:11.944
"D-J-A-N-G-O.

00:03:13.257 --> 00:03:14.377
The ‘D’ is silent."

00:03:15.104 --> 00:03:15.875
"I know."

00:03:17.066 --> 00:03:21.390
With "Kill Bill" being told out of chronological
order, the first assassin that The Bride takes

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:26.310
her revenge on isn't Vernita Green as shown
in the "Vol. 1" opening, but O-Ren Ishii.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.140
In the four years that The Bride was rendered
comatose by the cabal of global assassins,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.280
O-Ren seized power as one of the most powerful
Yakuza leaders in Japan.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.040
After The Bride cuts through O-Ren's private
army at a Tokyo restaurant, the two former

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.110
associates engage in their own private duel,
with O-Ren correctly predicting how long the

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.230
fight will last.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.629
While O-Ren is surprised that The Bride wields
a sword forged by the retired Hanzo, she estimates

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:51.970
that the impending duel won't last five minutes.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:59.590
O-Ren is correct in her prediction, but not
in the way that she anticipated.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.110
The Bride cuts off the top of her head, killing
her after a hard-fought battle.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:07.909
The length of the sword fight is exactly four
minutes and 59 seconds, fulfilling O-Ren's

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:08.909
lethal prophecy.

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:13.490
The most prominent storyline in Tarantino's
"Pulp Fiction" is one that follows hitmen

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.470
Vincent Vega and Jules Winnfield through Los
Angeles.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.169
While retrieving a briefcase belonging to
their employer Marsellus Wallace, Vincent

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:24.550
and Jules get into a gunfight with an unnamed
character looking to avenge his friends.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.900
Surprising the two assassins, the character
completely unloads a pistol at them — but

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.270
every bullet strikes the wall behind Vincent
and Jules instead.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.320
In an oversight that made it into the final
film, the bullet holes in the wall behind

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.560
Vincent and Jules are present before the shooting
even begins.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.710
This suggests that the scene of Vincent and
Jules talking in front of the bullet-ridden

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.410
wall was filmed before much of the following
sequence.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.160
The claims from Jules that divine intervention
saved him and Vincent from mortal harm is

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.283
a bit undercut by the knowledge that the bullet
holes were there all along.

00:04:50.917 --> 00:04:55.880
The opening title sequence to 1997's "Jackie
Brown" sets the tone for Tarantino's adaptation

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.870
of Elmore Leonard's 1992 novel "Rum Punch."

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:04.090
From its grainy, color-saturated cinematography
and Pam Grier's casting to the 1973 Bobby

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:09.914
Womack song "Across 110th Street," Tarantino
pays direct homage to '70s blaxploitation cinema.

00:05:09.914 --> 00:05:14.110
The memorable sequence would later be featured
in a direct callback by Tarantino in "Once

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.259
Upon a Time... in Hollywood."

00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:19.660
"Jackie Brown" opens with the titular protagonist
moving confidently through a brightly-colored

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.460
tiled hallway in LAX before breaking into
a run to board flight passengers on time at

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.460
the gate.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.789
The same hallway is featured in "Once Upon
a Time," when Rick Dalton and Cliff Booth

00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:32.140
make their triumphant return to Los Angeles
with Rick's new wife Francesca.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:36.500
In both sequences, the characters exude a
sense of cool as they move through the airport,

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:40.790
quietly hinting at their status quo before
their respective narratives kick into high gear.

00:05:41.002 --> 00:05:45.251
Given how the late actor and martial artist
is depicted in "Once Upon a Time," Tarantino

00:05:45.251 --> 00:05:47.980
has a complicated stance regarding the legacy
of Bruce Lee.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:53.060
However, Lee received a decidedly more favorable
nod in "Kill Bill: Volume 1" with The Bride's

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.280
choice in fashion during her stay in Tokyo.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.110
From her cruise through the city on her motorcycle
to her memorable sword fight in the House

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:04.027
of Blue Leaves, The Bride wears a black and
bright yellow tracksuit and helmet evocative of Lee.

00:06:04.027 --> 00:06:08.311
This iconic outfit is a visual homage to the
martial arts legend's similar ensemble in

00:06:08.311 --> 00:06:12.200
his posthumously released final film "Game
of Death" in 1978.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.590
The Bride's iteration of the yellow and black
outfit would become one of the more recognizable

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:20.000
images of the character, with her wearing
the tracksuit on the "Volume 1" theatrical poster.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.696
How would The Bride fare against Cliff Booth?

00:06:21.696 --> 00:06:24.066
Possibly better than Bruce Lee did.

00:06:28.738 --> 00:06:33.160
"Once Upon a Time ... In Hollywood" blends
real-life figures and true events in the 1960s

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:37.160
entertainment scene with fictional characters
and twists on established history, all in

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.009
service to Tarantino's love letter to 1969
Los Angeles.

00:06:41.009 --> 00:06:45.100
A prime example of the blur between actual
history and Tarantino's imagination occurs

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.840
when Rick Dalton takes an extended sojourn
in Italy to star in European films.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.870
Among the filmmakers that Rick works with
while in Europe is a real director, one amusingly

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.780
name-dropped in Tarantino's 2009 film "Inglourious
Basterds."

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.150
While infiltrating a Nazi film premiere in
Paris, Allied soldier Donny Donowitz uses

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.590
the alias Antonio Margheriti while posing
as an Italian director at the event.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:11.970
Antonio Margheriti is an actual Italian filmmaker
who directed feature films from 1960 to 1989.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.470
He is also credited in "Once Upon a Time...

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:18.102
In Hollywood" for casting Rick in the fictional
movie "Operazione Dyn-o-mite."

00:07:18.546 --> 00:07:20.980
"Margheritiiiiiiiiii."

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:22.910
"Once Upon a Time...

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.940
In Hollywood" is a lavish, painstakingly faithful
recreation of Los Angeles in 1969, drawing

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.639
from Tarantino's own memories of the city.

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:33.240
One of the most prominent elements he includes
in the film are the classic cars that Cliff

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.310
Booth drives around L.A., one belonging to
himself and the other to his best friend and

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:38.460
boss, Rick Dalton.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.970
In obtaining these throwback automobiles,
Tarantino revisited some of the cars he had

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:43.699
memorably used in his past projects.

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:48.720
Rick is chauffeured around the city in his
yellow 1966 Cadillac Coupe de Ville by Cliff

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.630
after receiving a DUI prior to the events
of the film.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:56.300
That car was previously driven by Mr. Blonde
in 1992's "Reservoir Dogs."

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:00.090
The actual car is owned by Michael Madsen,
who personally lent it to the production.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:04.650
Cliff himself, meanwhile, drives a blue 1968
Volkswagen Karmann Ghia — the same make

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.960
and model previously driven by The Bride in
"Kill Bill: Volume 2."

00:08:08.415 --> 00:08:12.620
Much like Alfred Hitchcock and other directors
before him, Tarantino has made it a point

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.740
to cameo in many of his movies.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:18.620
It's a fun tidbit for Tarantino fans to look
out for, and one where he might have a slight

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.360
advantage over other filmmakers.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:24.150
After all, Tarantino considered himself an
actor for many years, and has starred in films

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.240
like "From Dusk Till Dawn" and "Destiny Turns
on the Radio."

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.210
Appearing as Mr. Brown in "Reservoir Dogs,"
Tarantino delivered the film's opening riff

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.209
on Madonna, and was quickly dispatched.

00:08:33.209 --> 00:08:37.269
In "Pulp Fiction," the filmmaker played coffee-loving
Jimmie, who was willing to help with a body

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:40.830
disposal as long as it went down before his
wife Bonnie got home.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.570
Tarantino briefly popped up as the bar owner
in "Death Proof," and "Django Unchained" brought

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:48.940
along what might have been the most sizable
role he gave himself: an explosives obsessed...

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:50.000
Australian?

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.760
One overlooked role is in the closing segment
of "Four Rooms," in which he lays out the

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.800
terms of a bloody wager inspired by an old
"Alfred Hitchcock Presents" episode.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.910
More subtle Tarantino cameos include a voice
on an answering machine in "Jackie Brown,"

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:05.490
a dead Nazi in "Inglourious Basterds," and
narration for "The Hateful Eight."

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:07.570
Did he have a cameo in "Hollywood"?

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:10.920
Supposedly, that's him screaming "cut" at
the end of the Dalton commercial.

00:09:11.445 --> 00:09:12.445
"And cut."

00:09:12.445 --> 00:09:14.720
"Alright, this cigarette tastes like f---ing
s---."

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.470
And in "Kill Bill," some think he was one
of the Crazy 88.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:22.710
Though it isn't explicitly stated within the
films themselves, Tarantino has created familial

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.240
links between some of the figures in his filmography.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:29.550
Mr. Blonde's real name in "Reservoir Dogs"
is revealed to be Vic Vega, whom Tarantino

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.339
has claimed to be the brother of Vincent Vega
from "Pulp Fiction."

00:09:32.339 --> 00:09:35.950
In various interviews over the years, Tarantino
has alluded to the fact that he once had an

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:40.000
idea for a "Vega Brothers" movie that would
bring Michael Madsen and John Travolta’s

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.320
characters together — but like many Tarantino
ideas, it never came to fruition.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:48.454
Tarantino's penchant to weave hidden family
histories would later continue with his historical films.

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:52.960
In "Basterds," Michael Fassbender's British
intelligence officer Archie Hicox is related

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.519
to duplicitous outlaw English Pete Hicox in
"Hateful Eight."

00:09:56.519 --> 00:10:00.700
As for recurring names in Tarantino's initial
films, it's no secret that he wrote his earliest

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:05.360
scripts during overlapping periods, some with
the expectation they might not get made.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.860
It's unsurprising, then, that Jimmie in "Pulp
Fiction" and Mr. White in "Reservoir Dogs"

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:13.010
share the surname "Dimmick," and Tom Sizemore's
"Natural Born Killers” detective and Blonde's

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.790
parole officer in "Reservoir Dogs" are both
Scagnetti.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:20.779
What may have started as unintended script
shorthand, it seems, has become a Tarantino trademark.

00:10:21.214 --> 00:10:25.301
One of Tarantino's favorite collaborators
in recent years has been Kurt Russell, who

00:10:25.301 --> 00:10:28.260
appeared prominently in "The Hateful Eight"
and "Once Upon a Time...

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.260
In Hollywood."

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.310
The two first got together for "Death Proof",
with Russell playing against type as a serial

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:37.970
killer named Stuntman Mike who preys on young
women, using his own modified car as a murder weapon.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.200
Death Proof" contains several references to
Russell's own long-running career, all while

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.390
subverting audience expectations about his
tendency to play heroic roles.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:49.339
When Stuntman Mike introduces himself, he
reveals that he previously worked long ago

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:52.339
as a stuntman on the television series "The
Virginian."

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:56.339
As a child actor, one of Russell's earliest
roles was on an episode of "The Virginian,"

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:58.770
with the reference being a nod to Russell's
acting origins.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:02.639
A more subtle reference to Russell's career
in "Death Proof" is his character Jack Burton's

00:11:02.639 --> 00:11:06.339
white tank top from "Big Trouble in Little
China," hung up on the wall of the Austin

00:11:06.339 --> 00:11:08.199
dive bar where he stalks his prey.

